ice -> -0.13 pp core.\nChina overcapacity + property -> export price deflation -> [-] OECD core goods 0 to -0.5%/yr.\nBIS Box C: labour income lags capital-concentrated output -> [-] demand deficit -> -0.2 to -0.4 pp core.\nWEIGHTS (the whole answer). GDP-weighted OECD-38 core basket, with my 10-yr component rates: shelter 0.18 x 3.3 = 0.59; health/social care 0.12 x 3.2 = 0.38; other labour-intensive services 0.30 x 2.8 = 0.84; household-consumed financial/professional 0.10 x 1.3 = 0.13; core goods 0.25 x 0.5 = 0.13; communication/digital 0.05 x -1.0 = -0.05. Sum = 2.02%, plus 0.06 for the front-half electricity indirect = 2.08%/yr. Path: 2027-29 ~2.6-2.9%, 2030-33 ~2.0%, 2034-36 ~1.7%.\nCRITICAL WEIGHT FACT: electricity is EXCLUDED from US core CPI/PCE and from HICP core by construction, so the 4-6% power/construction/care skew reaches the LEVEL only through the ~2% energy cost share of service gross output. A regime of 5% electricity and -1% software therefore produces ~2.0% core, not 3%. P(OECD core avg ≥2.50% 2027-2036) = 0.30 — requires fiscal dominance, unanchored expectations, OR the care bottleneck clearing through prices instead of queues.\nCHINA conjunct, treated as a semi-independent object. Chain: property investment permanently -55/-60% from 2021 peak -> residence category (~18-20% of CPI) flat-to-negative; household income share stuck ~44% of GDP [semi-elasticity +0.08 pp CPI per +1 pp income share — no reflation channel]; pension subsidy already 10.1% of fiscal expenditure (2A) -> the state cannot buy down precautionary saving -> consumption weak; deliberate supply-side policy -> manufacturing capacity +5-7%/yr vs domestic demand +3-4%/yr -> PPI deflation persists into non-food goods CPI; -60M population 2026-2035 -> demand falls faster than capacity. Offsetting late-window arrow: working-age -1%/yr -> services wage pressure 2033-2036 [+0.5-0.8 pp]. 10-yr average P50 ≈ 1.15%. P(<1.50%) = 0.70; the NO tail is deliberate reflation/RMB depreciation pass-through or a pork cycle (a single 4% year adds 0.4 pp to the average).\nJOINT: conjuncts are NEGATIVELY correlated through the trade channel (Chinese deflation is itself the force holding OECD core goods at 0.5%), partly offset by the fragmentation case where tariffs break transmission; rho = -0.15. 0.30 x 0.70 = 0.21; cov = -0.15 x 0.21 = -0.032; joint = 0.178 -> 18.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.097Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 GDP-weighted health-and-social-care services price index (or, as a readable proxy, the US CPI medical-care-services index together with euro-area HICP health services) runs at or above 4.5% year-on-year for four consecutive quarters, with public-sector pay settlements in ISIC Q at or above 4%/yr in at least five of the G7.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my central monopsony arrow — the care bottleneck would be clearing through PRICES, not administered rationing. Core level path rises ~+0.35 pp, so CQ2 goes 18 -> 30 (first conjunct marginal 0.30 -> 0.48); CQ6-C +10 (11 -> 21) and CQ6-D -12 (34 -> 22); CQ1 rises 22 -> 31 via conjunct 2; CQ3 P50 rises ~+0.15 pp because the rationing GDP leak and the informal-carer labour-force exit shrink.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative announced-then-cancelled-or-impaired hyperscaler AI capex stays below 10% of announced buildouts AND net external AI software/API revenue exceeds 1.0x annualized AI compute D&A for four consecutive quarters (i.e. locked AI-Q4 is resolving NO).\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the 2031-2033 bust from the middle of every 2032-2036 measurement window. CQ3 P50 1.6 -> 2.1, P90 2.7 -> 3.2; CQ4 P50 1.8 -> 2.2 (investment demand holds r* up); CQ6-A 11 -> 22 and CQ6-D 34 -> 18; CQ5 falls 34 -> 26 as the leveraged-unwind shock source t